# Accessibility and design

- Semantic HTML first: `header`, `main`, `nav`, `section` with `aria-labelledby`, `article`, `ul`/`li`, `button` for actions, `form` and `label`. Use ARIA only when HTML can't express it.
- Heading hierarchy: one `h1` (the app title), `h2` per panel, `h3` per post.
- Everything works with the keyboard. Focus is always visible (`focus-visible:` ring tokens) and never removed without a replacement.
- Every control has a visible label. Errors are linked with `aria-describedby`, invalid inputs have `aria-invalid="true"`.
- Async status: loading regions have `aria-busy`. Status messages use `role="status"` (polite). Failures use `role="alert"`.
- Decorative icons and avatars are `aria-hidden="true"`.
- Color contrast meets WCAG 2.2 AA in light and dark mode. Color is never the only signal (pair it with text or an icon).
- Respect `prefers-color-scheme` (dark mode via tokens) and `prefers-reduced-motion` (`motion-safe:` for transitions and pulses).
- Design tokens live in `@theme` in `src/index.css`. Use the token utilities, never hard-coded hex values in components.
- Responsive from 360 px: master-detail on `md` and up, separate views below.
- Loading uses skeletons shaped like the content. Empty states have an icon, a title and guidance.
